From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HourlySword <hourlysword.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:03:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Software renderer for the FunKey S (no GPU), and headless
 screenshots from it

rt/render_soft.cpp implements render.h on the CPU and replaces render.cpp in ISAAC_FUNKEY builds (it builds on PC
too). Modelled on the PSP's renderer: one thread, the frame drawn right after the tick that built the snapshot.
- The scene is drawn at the stage's own resolution (363x272 with genpsp/) and scaled once to the output (area
  averaging; vertical pass first, with NEON on ARM): 240x180 in the middle of the FunKey's 240x240 screen, or the
  whole screen (g_softStretch). The game's layers and most sprites are then copied 1:1, not resampled.
- Shapes, texts and morphs rasterized with raster.cpp at the scale they're shown at (1/32-octave keys, an 8 MB cache,
  least recently used dropped); drawn 1:1 (mirroring included) when the transform allows, else resampled (bilinear).
- Each graphic's rows indexed once into runs (opaque stretches copied, the rest blended, gaps skipped): a room is a
  dozen stage-sized, mostly transparent layers. BitmapData runs follow BitmapData::version (SnapNode::bmVersion).
- Large graphics stretched or turned but still (same transform as the previous frame), and graphics far bigger than
  the stage, are rasterized at their transform where they show (raster.cpp: rasterizeSrcAt) and copied 1:1.
- BitmapData drawn from their own pixels (SnapNode::bmPx, as on the PSP; snapshot.cpp copies nothing for them).
- Colour transforms per pixel as render.cpp's shader computes them; masks with an 8-bit stencil.
- Not done, as on the PSP: blur filters, interpolation, preloading, the focus "ink" (a frame instead).
- Diagnostics text, the version picker and the loading screen drawn on the output with the 5x7 font.

main.cpp: in a FunKey build, headless mode renders without a window or SDL video (--headless --size WxH; "shot"
writes PPM or BMP), and "OFKMEM dbegin" / "OFKMEM draw" lines bracket each frame for qemu's armprof.
Testing hooks: ISAAC_SOFT_CACHE_KB, ISAAC_SOFT_STRETCH, ISAAC_SOFT_RASTERLOG, ISAAC_SOFT_DRAWLOG=<tick>.

Checked: screenshots against render.cpp (Mesa, same ticks, layers on the CPU): rooms within about 1 level in 255 per
channel, menus 2.5-4.5 (no blurred shadows). Under qemu, drawing a floor 1 room is 5.0 M ARM instructions per frame
(median), floor 2 6.2 M; the logic still plays the same as the PC reference (every enemy's state at every tick).
